(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609030900-aiueos-pure-kotoba-native-programme")
 (:adr/title "AIUEOS pure Kotoba/Amu native: what thirty-one parallel streams measured, and what K16 still runs in C")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-03")
 (:adr/body "# AIUEOS pure Kotoba/Amu native — the 2026-09-02/03 programme

## What was asked

Make AIUEOS a Kotoba native OS: boot, NIC, HTTPS and Qwen inference all
compiled from Kotoba by Amu to x86-64, with no runtime delegation to
external C/C++ or handwritten assembly. Four acceptance conditions were
set by the owner: PCI/MMIO/DMA emitted directly by Amu; f32/SIMD/AVX2
fused dequantize+dot kernels rather than a C matvec called from Kotoba;
boot, network and TLS as Kotoba/Amu artifacts; and a foreign-code
negative gate that fails the build rather than relying on attention.

The owner also corrected the definition in advance: toolchain-generated
fixed stubs (interrupt entry, context switch) are admissible when Amu
emits them reproducibly. The line is delegation, not the presence of
machine code nobody typed.

## What the programme actually produced

Thirty-one streams ran in parallel across sixteen repositories. The
substantive results, each with the evidence that carries it:

### Compiler capability, all executed rather than asserted

- f32 scalar arithmetic and every int/float conversion reach native on
  both ISAs, verified bit-for-bit against the KIR oracle by executing
  real machine code (81 assertions).
- kernel-dot-f32 emits an AVX2 arm and a scalar arm that are
  bit-identical by construction, both executed under QEMU and agreeing
  with the oracle (4B800004). A Kotoba kernel can now enable XSAVE/YMM
  itself through kernel-read/write-cr4 and kernel-xsetbv.
- Fused dequantize+dot lands for Q8_0, Q4_K and Q6_K with both arms
  executed. The four IQ formats are declared, oracled and refused by
  name; their codebook grids are expressible because the literal pool
  reaches the kernel-object route.
- Memory grew from two widths at two window sizes to 32 operations
  across four widths and four tiers, plus a slice carrier that lowers to
  a scaled load with zero context callbacks and can be passed as a
  function parameter.
- Fences, rdtsc, general atomics, swapgs, port I/O and MSR access exist;
  interrupt entry is authored in Kotoba with a toolchain-generated
  prologue and was executed by taking a real #UD.
- The fuel budget is 64 bits. 2.2e9 fuel was spent on a CPU past the old
  2^31 ceiling, with a same-bytes-but-the-budget control stopping where
  the arithmetic says.

### AIUEOS

- A foreign-code negative gate exists and refuses. On the K16 link list
  it reports its counts rather than a verdict, and the pure-native
  profile refuses the loader by name.
- Every committed Kotoba object is attested to one compiler revision:
  unattested went from 40 to 0, and the rebuilt image boots with every
  parity marker.
- The GGUF admission, the TLS record layer and AES-128-GCM, the RTL8125
  driver, the Murakumo device-worker canonical form, a streaming
  SHA-256, the Qwen tokenizer, and the Qwen dequant/dot/matvec/norm/
  activation/attention/recurrent kernels are Kotoba objects. Several
  were flipped so the C calls them; the rest are linked and compared.
- A BOOTX64.EFI compiled from Kotoba boots under OVMF, calls firmware
  protocol methods with six arguments, allocates pages, writes them,
  walks the memory map, hashes 176 bytes with SHA-256 inside the image,
  and jumps to a function whose address it took.

### Six silent defects found, and what they have in common

Each of these returned the same value as a check that ran and found
nothing wrong. That is the shape this workspace already forbids in
prose; the programme found six instances of it in one day.

1. A let body with more than one form kept only the first. The rule
   existed in validate-expr and ran last, so it measured the already
   truncated form and admitted it.
2. A bounded store answered with an undefined register on native while
   the oracle answered with the stored operand.
3. A register-allocator spill-slot collision froze a loop counter at its
   entry value, so four committed objects never terminated. One of them
   was linked.
4. The --fuel flag was validated and then discarded on two artifact
   routes; artifacts at 512 and at 1048576 were byte-identical.
5. A QEMU smoke reported a serial log from a kernel that was not in the
   tree, because the build failed and the previous artifacts survived.
6. A dead guest and a known flake were the same observation: after a #UD
   the firmware halts, QEMU never exits, and the retry loop keyed on the
   timeout status.

### Measurement lessons worth keeping

- A fixture can state a limit by being one past it, naming no constant a
  source search can reach. CI found the fifth statement of the fuel
  ceiling that way.
- Equal weights see no geometry: a dequant probe whose weights are all
  equal passes with a wrong nibble, a wrong byte and a wrong scale. Two
  fixtures had to be rebuilt before a broken emitter could be caught.
- A golden that counts writes per accumulator passes for an assignment
  that writes each twice, which is what the wrong lane assignment also
  does. Pin the sequence, not the histogram.
- Byte movement is not evidence of a defect. Sixty-nine of seventy-four
  objects moved for a codegen change that reached four.
- A check whose search space grows is stronger at the same finding
  count: self-tail loops went from 301 to 423 while findings went to
  zero.

## Decision

The definition stands as the owner stated it. Progress against it is
measured by the K16 pure-native gate, whose foreign count is the number
that matters and which currently reports the C that remains. Object
attestation is complete; the compiler capability gap that blocked
NIC/DMA/f32/SIMD is closed; the loader is proven in parts and not yet
composed.

## Consequences

- The K16 image still runs its inference and its loader in C. Nothing in
  this programme changed what K16 boots, and no measurement here was
  taken on K16 hardware.
- matvec_range still runs the C every token, so the 0.036 tok/s figure
  is unchanged and untouched.
- uefi/main.c is 1,214 lines byte-unchanged, and the pure-native profile
  refuses it by name.
- The remaining foreign objects are the C, and are other work.

## Status of the four acceptance conditions

1. PCI/MMIO/DMA emitted by Amu: the primitives exist and the RTL8125
   driver is six Kotoba objects whose register transcript matches the C
   under QEMU. Not run on RTL8125 silicon.
2. f32/SIMD fused kernels: three quantization formats emit with both
   arms executed; the four dominant IQ formats do not yet. No cycle
   ratio is claimed from TCG; the defensible number is a static
   instruction count of roughly 4 to 6 times.
3. Boot/network/TLS as Kotoba artifacts: the TLS record layer and
   AES-GCM are flipped and executed; the loader boots and does four of
   the C loader's jobs; the model read, the embedded-release path and
   BootNext are untouched.
4. Foreign-code negative gate: exists, refuses, and is wired to the
   build.

## What the next session should read first

scratchpad results.md carries every stream's report with its evidence.
The single most load-bearing correction to any brief is that a minimal
Kotoba kernel fixture is 110,872 bytes because segment 1 is fixed, which
inverts the loader stage order: fs must come before PT_LOAD placement,
or placement must use the 8,560-byte user-image ELF.")))
